    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 020520\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1020 PM MST Tue Sep 1 2026\n\n.UPDATE..06Z Aviation Discussion\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Temperatures should remain in a near to below normal category\n  for the first week of September, resulting in widespread Minor \n  Heat Risk and localized areas of Moderate Heat Risk.\n\n- Drier conditions will prevail through the middle of the work \n  week before potentially another period of wet weather \n  materializes late week into the weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nAnother strong W-E gradient in surface dewpoints was noted this \nmorning in the vicinity of the CO River Valley, with dewpoint \ntemperatures in the 40s across much of Southeast CA and 60s to lower \n70s across Southwest AZ. The higher dewpoints across our eastern CWA \nare indicative of the better low level moisture that remains in \nplace, however, the 12Z PSR sounding reveals a very dry layer \naround/just above 500 mb, thanks to the sustained southwesterly to \nwesterly flow aloft. With daytime heating, anticipate further \ndrying of the low levels as the drier air aloft mixes down into \nthe boundary layer. However, there will be enough moisture to \nsupport isolated deep moist convection over prominent terrain \nfeatures of Southwest AZ (primarily Central/Northeastern La Paz \nCounty) and perhaps the Tabletop Mountains of Southwestern \nPinal/Southern Maricopa Counties. The intensity of convection will\nbe reduced compared to yesterday, with a reduction in instability\nand the addition of subsidence, which is evidenced by a strong \nmidlevel inversion present in GFS bufr soundings and the 12Z PSR \nsounding. Differential heating across the quasi dry-line in \nSouthwest AZ may provide a focus for convective initiation as it \nprogresses eastward this afternoon. This, coupled with lingering \neffective bulk shear around 25-30 kts presents the possibility for\nsome of the La Paz County storms to organize and become strong. A\nvery isolated risk for a severe thunderstorm or two cannot be \nentirely ruled out. Fields of cumulus are already visible on \nsatellite imagery early this afternoon, stretching from far \nSoutheast CA through the CO River Valley and into La Paz County. \nWe will be keeping a close eye on this area for further \ndevelopment. \n\nEnsembles remain in excellent agreement that southwesterly flow \naloft with deep Pacific troughing along the West Coast will be \nmaintained through at least the middle of the work week, keeping the \nsubtropical high suppressed and displaced well east of the region. \nIn fact, a compact PV anomaly/area of strongly negative midlevel \nheight anomalies has dived southward and can be seen in midlevel wv \nimagery early this afternoon starting to move onshore along the \nNorCal Coast, resulting in a restrengthening of southwesterly flow \naloft and further drying through Wednesday. This will lead to \nanother quiet day across our forecast area with any isolated \nconvection relegated to the higher terrain of northern and \nsoutheastern AZ. This will also lead to an uptick in daytime\nwinds, particularly across Southeast CA/Southwest AZ. Ensemble \nmean H5 heights will remain in a seasonable range, around 587-590\ndam. Mean 850 mb temperatures between 22C-26C (between the\n20th-60th percentiles of climatology) will support surface \ntemperatures within a few degrees of the daily normals through \nThursday, with lower desert highs generally ranging from the upper\n90s to around 105F, following morning lows from the upper 70s to \nmiddle 80s. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nThe forecast becomes a bit more muddy heading into the end of this \nweek and through the weekend. The NHC shows Tropical Storm Marie \nhas formed well of the coast of Southwestern Mexico. Most \nforecast models show this storm curving out into open waters by\nthis weekend, however there is good evidence that at least some \ntropical moisture will get pulled up into Mexico and making it as \nfar north as southern AZ as early as Thursday/Thursday night. \nRoughly 90% of the GEFS and EPS members show PWATs increasing from\n1.5\" on Thursday to around 1.8-2.0\" over portions of southcentral\nand southwest AZ by Friday or Saturday. Given this likely \nincrease in moisture, NBM PoPs have increased to around 40-50% \nwith the initial moisture push late Thursday into Friday. \nThereafter, PoPs remain quite low in a 20-30% range through the \nweekend which is most likely due to uncertainty in timing and \ncoverage of rainfall within the deterministic model output. \n\nAlthough there will be adequate moisture return, the positioning \nof the ridge/trough features over the west will play a key role in\nhow much rainfall may actually materialize. If the ridge \nstrengthens too much and retrogrades wwd, then the focus for \nheavier rainfall may be much further west into SoCal and north \ninto UT and CO as indicated by the latest ECMWF and GFS runs. It \nwill likely be at least 2 or 3 more days until we have a good idea\non what will happen late this week, and any forecast that does \nnot show a range of possible outcomes should be disregarded at \nthis time. \n\nUncertainty in the temperature forecast by this weekend is also \napparent as indicated by large (8-10 degree) spreads within the \nNBM inner quartile range. Once our region does begin to draw in \nthe Pacific moisture, temperatures will likely dip down to around \n3-5 degrees below normal by the end of this week. However, as \nridging aloft attempts to build over the area this weekend, \ntemperatures may warm closer to normal by Sunday or Monday. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0520Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected through Wednesday\nafternoon. Winds will follow light familiar diurnal trends with\nperiods of VRB conditions overnight and some occasional afternoon\ngusts in the mid teens to around 20 kt. A slower-than-usual \ntransition from east to west may produce a few hours of southerly \ncross winds at KPHX and KDVT, with perhaps a window of directions \n160V230 before westerly flow takes over. There are hints of TS \nactivity moving within the vicinity of the terminals during the \nlate afternoon to early evening hours, but no mention is included \nin the TAF at this time as rain chances are too low ~10%. \nGenerally clear skies tonight will give way to a FEW low- level \nclouds during the back half of the period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours.\nWinds will favor a westerly component at KIPL, while south-\nsouthwesterly to southwesterly flow will be common at KBLH. Breezy\nconditions with gusts upwards of 20-25 kt will develop at each\nterminal during the back half of the forecast window. Skies will\nbe mostly clear through Wednesday evening.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier conditions will prevail through Thursday with only isolated\nchances for showers and thunderstorms mostly relegated to\nprominent terrain features across AZ. Better chances for wetting\nrains area-wide return late this week into this weekend. Afternoon\nminimum humidities will range from 7-15% across Southeast CA and\nbetween 15-35% for much of Southern AZ through the next few days.\nThis will follow poor to fair overnight recoveries across the\nwestern deserts and good to excellent overnight recoveries across\nthe eastern districts. Daytime winds will remain elevated out of\nthe south to southwest, with the strongest gusts in Southeast CA\nand along the Lower Colorado River Valley peaking between 20-30\nmph during the afternoon/early evening. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Whittock\nLONG TERM...Salerno\nAVIATION...RW\nFIRE WEATHER...Whittock\n",
